#
# hosts -- the host name database, in the traditional format:
#
#	address		name [aliases...]
#
# Read by gethostbyname()/gethostbyaddr() (os/net/netdb.c).  It is consulted
# BEFORE the network: a dotted quad first, then this file, and only then the
# DNS resolver -- and the resolver only at all if /etc/resolv.conf exists.  So
# a name here answers with no packet, and a name in neither this file nor the
# DNS is HOST_NOT_FOUND.
#
# The names of this machine and of its SLIP peer belong here rather than in the
# DNS, for two reasons: they must resolve before the link is up (rc.net starts
# slip after ifconfig), and the nameserver named in /etc/resolv.conf is itself
# 10.0.0.1, so a machine that needed DNS to find its peer could never ask.
#
# The first name on a line is the canonical one and the rest are aliases; a
# lookup matches any of them and answers with the canonical name.
#
# The entries below match /etc/rc.net's default SLIP configuration: this
# machine is 10.0.0.2 and the host at the far end of the serial line is
# 10.0.0.1.  Change them together with IFADDR in rc.net, the nameserver line in
# /etc/resolv.conf, and /etc/hostname.
#
# /etc/hostname holds `c900.localnet', which is the canonical name below on
# purpose: gethostname() has to resolve to this machine's own interface address
# and not to 127.0.0.1.  hunt's client asks its own name to decide which network
# to look for a game driver on, and a hostname that resolved to loopback made it
# unable to find a server running on this very machine.
#
127.0.0.1	localhost
10.0.0.2	c900.localnet c900 coherent
10.0.0.1	peer.localnet peer slip-peer
